ah i mean marrying the queen in 1947  uh that's a long long time ago he lived  a great many years  and a lot of those years were put in to  service uh  as one of the pillars of the royal  family one of the most important  institutions of the united kingdom and  of course the commonwealth  around the world as to whether  the uk uh is going to be united in its  uh  attitudes to prince philip what of  course it isn't um this is a country  which has  uh you know republican leanings from  certain sections of the population  i think many young people the left-wing  press  uh will perhaps think that his passing  needs no  more or less mourning than the death of  any 99 year old man but certainly in the  right-wing press  perhaps more conservative older britons  they will be  uh i think treating him with a lot more  reverence  going into his various uh qualities and  and mourning him  uh over the coming days and weeks and of  course we will hear  uh the kind of things that uh uh well  the kind of things from the british  government that we have just heard from  boris johnson and we can we can listen  to his uh his comments now  prince philip earned the affection of  generations  here in the united kingdom across the  commonwealth  and around the world he was the  longest-serving consort  in history one of the last surviving  people in this country  to have served in the second world war  at cape matapan  where he was mentioned in dispatches for  bravery  and in the invasion of sicily where he  saved his ship  by his quick thinking and from that  conflict  he took an ethic of service that he  applied  throughout the unprecedented changes of  the post-war era  like the expert carriage driver that he  was  he helped to steer the royal family and  the monarchy  so that it remains an institution  indisputably vital  to the balance and happiness of our  national life  uh rory the duke of edinburgh was very  old he died at the age of 99 so this  won't be a a great shock to many people  and  of course these funerals are planned uh  years in advance  but nobody really could have foreseen  the fact that uh  prince philip would have died in the  middle of a global pandemic how are they  going to  to hold this uh pomp and pageantry uh  the royal send-off while the country is  still in lockdown  well i think it will be a very different  uh funeral  than the ones that palace organizers  will have been preparing for pre-covet  of course  since covert hit they have had to come  up with a plan b  and what we know plan b won't entail is  the kind of things that we're used to  seeing  in royal funerals so processions through  the streets  crowds lining those streets waving flags  packed in tightly  next to each other perhaps a coffin  lying in a cathedral  with people processing past it and  paying their respects that  is most likely going to be impossible  at the moment in the uk because of coba  restrictions  funerals are limited to 30 people  maximum now that is going to endure i  think for  for at least a few weeks perhaps another  couple of months or so  so if we have uh prince phillips funeral  within that time it is likely to be much  much smaller and much  less public than the organizers have  been hoping for  so it is almost certainly going to take  place at  windsor castle and be attended by  a much smaller number of people perhaps  just close  family of course it also raises  questions about whether family overseas  at the moment  prince harry and meghan markle who have  had their differences with the royal  family  as we know over recent months whether  they will be in attendance these are  questions we don't know the answer to  yet  okay rory challenge there live from  london  rory thank you very much indeed  well let's take a closer look at the  life of the duke of  edinburgh prince philip was born on june  10 1921  in corfu to greek and danish royal  families  he was known as prince philip of greece  and denmark  well due to his mother's 