d and it was philip  who  came in with that dash and panache that  he's got  um that helped change things and of  course  he ruffled a lot of feathers he made a  lot of enemies uh  doing this but in in so doing he  modernized  uh the house of windsor which would  otherwise have been  in a very much more difficult situation  his  mother-in-law the queen mother who had  been queen elizabeth to  the previous king king george vi uh  was a conservative uh backward-looking  figure  uh determined that she should have  control of her daughter  queen elizabeth and philip managed to  brush her  to one side and allow queen elizabeth  to be her own woman and that is really  his greatest contribution  it did quite well his life and us were  hearing in tributes coming in  from from presidents of prime ministers  around the world  his life really has been defined by  service and and royal duty but  he did have a very distinguished  military career of his own and it must  have been very  difficult to set that aside for  a role in which he spent most of his  life walking  several paces behind his wife  it broke his heart he thought that he  had a good chance  that king george vi queen elizabeth's  father would live at least for another  20 years it would give him a chance  to rise up in the royal navy he was an  enthusiastic sailor he was a military  man  he enjoyed the challenge uh the the  manly world  that the being on her ship at sea  demanded  and he was particularly good at it he  was competent at it  and uh to suddenly have this career  ripped away from him  and then as you say uh to have to walk  two steps behind  the woman that was his wife was very  difficult what i will say is  that he was born royal and he knew that  if you became  prince consort that that was your job  two steps behind  uh quite it's but he he fulfilled that  duty he he never wavered from it as much  as he would have liked her dislikes that  he he  carried that role and function to the  very end  and he was an important uh support  for uh queen elizabeth uh not just in in  the roles he  he performed but also to help her steady  the ship through what's been  quite a turbulent rain it has to be seen  uh  the the royal family has endured many  challenges not just the abdication the  death of princess diana and it's  it seems it's going through another  turbulent time right now with uh  the story of uh prince harry and meghan  markle  well he was able to discipline people to  be able to  call on the queen's authority the queen  said you have to run my family for me  because i'm  uh running matters of state and so  he was able to keep a relatively strong  discipline  um on the younger royals i mean  things happened you know princess diana  prince charles you know the  the difficulties that they've had with  virtually all the children  um you can't uh you can't  escape that in any family but what he  was able to do was he was able to bring  up an order and discipline so that if  something went wrong the somehow a  corrective could be made  um simply because he would knock heads  together he would insist  that the royal family came first and  even if you were a prince or  princess your duty was to the crown  to the queen if you like but to the  crown that's your job  that's what you do and he was a  wonderful sergeant major figure in that  way  okay a royal biographer christopher  wilson thank you so much for  uh for shedding light and giving more  context uh  to uh the life of the duke of edinburgh  who has  passed away at the age of 99.  let's move to other news now and we're  heading to myanmar where there are  reports of tense fighting  as security forces crack down

